\section{What the Table Minimizes}

Blindell's ``Optimal Pattern Selection with Dynamic Programming'' describes how TWIG separates match discovery, bottom-up cost computation, and top-down recovery \cite[section~3.6.2]{blindell2016instruction}. The separation matters: a matching pattern may ask for operands that the target cannot produce, or for an operand form whose extra cost makes the pattern a bad choice. Also, solving every subtree for the same preferred goal is not enough. The table stores a best derivation for each goal the parent might request.

Let $C(v,g)$ be the minimum cost of implementing the subtree at $v$ with result form $g$. For a matching rule $r$ with result $g$, let $F(v,r)$ be its frontier: pairs $(u,h)$ of descendant positions and required forms. For the chain-free grammar used here, every $u$ is a proper descendant. With additive rule weights $w$, compute
\[
 C(v,g)=\min_{\substack{r\text{ matches at }v\\r\text{ yields }g}}
 \left(w(r)+\sum_{(u,h)\in F(v,r)} C(u,h)\right).
\]
An empty collection of candidates gives infinity; an empty frontier contributes zero. Store the winning rule with each finite cost. Compare candidates only after checking that each frontier entry is finite. In particular, finding a structural match does not justify running its action.

\section{Choosing How to Represent a Condition}

Suppose the IR contains a pure word comparison $\mathrm{lt}$, a conditional value $\mathrm{sel}$, and a wrapping negation $\mathrm{neg}$. The selector may represent a comparison either as a word boolean $B$ or in a predicate register $P$. Ordinary word values have goal $W$. Here $B$ promises a word boolean, not merely an arbitrary $W$; no conversion between the goals is implicit. Use the following invented costs, which model a target where writing a comparison into the predicate bank is expensive.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{clr}
Rule & Production & Weight \\
$a$ & $W\gets\mathrm{arg}$ & 0 \\
$b$ & $B\gets\mathrm{lt}(W,W)$ & 1 \\
$p$ & $P\gets\mathrm{lt}(W,W)$ & 5 \\
$s$ & $W\gets\mathrm{sel}(B,W,W)$ & 3 \\
$t$ & $W\gets\mathrm{sel}(P,W,W)$ & 1 \\
$f$ & $W\gets\mathrm{sel}(\mathrm{lt}(W,W),W,W)$ & 5 \\
$n$ & $W\gets\mathrm{neg}(W)$ & 4 \\
$j$ & $W\gets\mathrm{neg}(\mathrm{sel}(P,W,W))$ & 2
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

Rule $j$ is a machine operation that selects and negates under a predicate. It covers two IR operators without taking the output of the select as an input. Thus its first frontier goal is at the comparison grandchild. Rule $f$ instead covers a select and its comparison without producing a standalone boolean. Neither tile is required to use the cheapest local implementation of an operator inside it.

