\section{Price the Alternatives, Then Recover Only One}

Set $k=\mathrm{lt}(a,b)$, $s_0=\mathrm{sel}(k,c,d)$, and $v=\mathrm{neg}(s_0)$, where the four argument leaves are distinct tree occurrences. Only finite entries and their candidates appear below.

\begin{verbatim}
node          goal   candidate costs              recorded
a,b,c,d       W      a: 0                         a: 0
k             B      b: 1 + 0 + 0                 b: 1
k             P      p: 5 + 0 + 0                 p: 5
s0            W      s: 3 + 1 + 0 + 0             s: 4 [1]
                     t: 1 + 5 + 0 + 0
                     f: 5 + 0 + 0 + 0 + 0
v             W      n: 4 + 4                     j: 7 [2]
                     j: 2 + 5 + 0 + 0

recover(v,W) -> j
    recover(k,P) -> p after retrieving a,b
    retrieve c,d
    execute j using P,c,d
\end{verbatim}

\noindent\textbf{[1]} At the select, the smallest local weight belongs to $t$ and the largest pattern is $f$. Neither wins. The word-boolean select costs four as a whole, and that record is useful if a parent demands the select result in a word.

\noindent\textbf{[2]} At the negation, the best tile skips the stored choice for $s_0$. It also asks for the expensive predicate rather than the cheap word boolean at $k$. The emitted sequence computes the predicate and uses it in the select--negate instruction. It emits neither a word-boolean comparison nor a standalone select. The records are answers to possible demands, not instructions to execute for every node.

As a regression test, disable $p$ but leave the shape for $j$ in the matcher. The predicate entry at $k$ becomes infinite. Rules $t$ and $j$ still match structurally but neither can now be selected. The result should cost eight and recover via $n$, $s$, and $b$.

\section{Why the Local Minima Compose}

In \Cref{fig:visual-267}, the winning root tile covers both the negation and the select. Recovery therefore never asks for the standalone \(W\) result at \(s_0\).

