The postorder invariant is that every completed entry is the optimum for that node and goal. Cut any derivation at its first tile. Its disjoint frontier subtrees have fixed operand goals, and replacing each derivation there with a cheaper one for the same goal cannot change the enclosing tile's legality. The recurrence therefore gives a lower bound on each derivation. Conversely, joining the winning tile and winning frontier derivations attains the computed sum. Recovery follows those witnesses, moving to a proper descendant at every step. For a fixed grammar the candidate and frontier counts per node are bounded, so the computation with precomputed matches takes linear time in the tree size.

Blindell's ``DP Versus LR Parsing'' qualification is that code generation does not always have this optimal substructure. If two operands compete for the same fixed register, the cheapest ways to obtain them separately might not be usable together. Likewise a weighted sum here does not predict every schedule or spill. Add goals only for interface properties that really make frontier choices independent, or treat the chosen cover as optimal under the selector's model rather than optimal machine code.
